---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import W from "../data/work.json";

const FAM = { blazevic: "Blažević", zubrinic: "Žubrinić", papic: "Papić",
              prpic: "Prpić", kalanj: "Kalanj", boras: "Boras",
              sestan: "Šestan", vukelic: "Vukelić" };
const reg = W.rows.filter((r) => r.from === "register");
const tree = W.rows.filter((r) => r.from === "tree");
const byFam = Object.entries(
  W.rows.reduce((a, r) => (r.family ? ((a[r.family] = (a[r.family] || 0) + 1), a) : a), {})
).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
---
<Base title="What They Did"
  eyebrow={`${W.rows.length} occupations — ${W.fromRegister} read off records, ${W.fromTree} carried by the tree`}
  dek="The family tree records a trade for sixteen people out of twelve hundred. The registers record one for almost everybody, in a column this archive had been reading past.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Both the Latin and the Croatian parish forms carry a
    <em>conditio</em> or <em>stalež</em> column, and the priest filled it in — for the parents, for
    the godparents, and for the witnesses. It is the only place in these documents where a person is
    described by something other than their name and their kin.</p>
    <p>This archive had been reading past it. Where an occupation surfaced it went into prose on one
    person's page and nowhere else, which meant it could not be counted or compared. <strong>The
    comparison is the point.</strong></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Two families, two worlds, twenty miles apart</h2>
    <div class="grid two">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The Žubrinići hold offices</h3>
        <ul class="plain">
          <li><strong>Confiniarius</strong> — a man of the <a href={u("/frontier/")}>Military
            Frontier</a>, Šumećica N° 24, 1834</li>
          <li><strong>Confiniarius ex Pago Dubrava N° 29</strong> — and so is
            <strong>Marcus Orešković</strong>, whose daughter married into this line. Both sides of
            that marriage are Frontier households.</li>
          <li><strong>Vigiliarum Magister pensionatus</strong> — master of the watch, pensioned</li>
          <li><strong>Silvarum Custos</strong> — forest warden, Šumećica</li>
          <li><strong>Custos Carcerum</strong> — keeper of the prison, Otočac, 1845</li>
          <li><strong>Forest Ranger</strong> — Otočac, the 1837 generation</li>
          <li><strong>kontrolor gradske daće</strong> — municipal excise controller, Senj, 1910</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem">Six salaried posts, four of them in a single parish
        register, three of them in <em>one entry</em>. These are people the state paid.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The Blaževići carry things</h3>
        <ul class="plain">
          <li><strong>nadničar</strong> — day labourer. Toma Blažević, Senj, at his death in 1907</li>
          <li><strong>seljaci</strong> — peasants. Toma and Tereza at Smokvica 114, 1884</li>
          <li><strong>selj.</strong> — the same, Jurko and Tereza, 1895</li>
          <li><strong>težak</strong> — labourer. Josip Papić, Senj, 1891</li>
          <li><strong>trhonoša, bremenar, gostioničar</strong> — porter, burden-carrier, innkeeper</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem">No office, no pension, no rank. The
        <a href={u("/papic/")}>Papići</a> carry sacks in the port; the Blaževići work by the day.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:1.4rem">These families are <strong>twenty miles apart</strong> and they
    married each other. <a href={u("/hedviga/")}>Hedviga</a> descends from both — from a household
    that held the keys to the Otočac gaol and from a household whose head was entered, at his death,
    as a man who worked by the day. <a href={u("/frontier/")}>The Frontier →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Added 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2>The offices did not travel</h2>
    <p class="lede">Australian electoral rolls give an occupation <em>and</em> a street address for
    every adult, every roll year, from 1913. For the branch that went to
    <a href={u("/port-pirie/")}>Port Pirie</a> that turns a family this archive knew only by name
    into a family it knows by trade — and the answer is not what the Croatian record would lead you
    to expect.</p>

    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Occupation</th><th>Where</th><th>Year</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Arthur Leslie Zubrinich</strong></td><td><strong>Ironmoulder</strong></td>
          <td class="dim">2 Daisy St, Port Pirie East</td><td class="mono">1941, 1943</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Keith Leo Zubrinich</strong></td><td><strong>Moulder</strong></td>
          <td class="dim">9 Queen Street, Port Pirie</td><td class="mono">1939</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Edward Malcolm Zubrinich</td><td>Labourer</td>
          <td class="dim">65 Queen Street, Solomontown</td><td class="mono">1939</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maurice Joseph Zubrinich</td><td>Labourer</td>
          <td class="dim">56 Simpson Road, Port Pirie</td><td class="mono">1939</td></tr>
        <tr><td>John Stephen Zubrinich</td><td>Labourer</td>
          <td class="dim">Whyalla</td><td class="mono">1939</td></tr>
        <tr><td>John William Zubrinich</td><td>Labourer</td>
          <td class="dim">Wolfram Street, Broken Hill</td><td class="mono">1913</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Catherine Zubrinich</td><td class="dim">Domestic duties</td>
          <td class="dim">Wolfram Street, Broken Hill</td><td class="mono">1913</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Austa Louise Zubrinich</td><td class="dim">Domestic duties</td>
          <td class="dim">21 St Ann's Terrace, Glenelg</td><td class="mono">1939</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p style="margin-top:1.4rem">In Lika these people held <strong>offices</strong> — warden of the
    forest, master of the watch, keeper of the prison, and a rank the register writes as
    <em>Confiniarius</em>. At Port Pirie, two generations later, they are
    <strong>labourers and moulders in a lead smelter</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>The offices did not travel.</strong> What a Frontier household held was a position in
    a system, and the system did not exist in South Australia — so the family arrived as what the
    smelter needed. The one man who climbed is <strong>Arthur Leslie, an ironmoulder</strong>, which
    is a skilled trade and not a labouring one.</p>
    <p class="dek">Two small things the addresses give away. <strong>Edward Malcolm at 65 Queen
    Street and Keith Leo at 9 Queen Street</strong> are on the same street in 1939, and Solomontown
    is where their father died. And <strong>John William and Catherine</strong> are at the same
    Broken Hill address in 1913 — a mining town four hundred kilometres inland, which is where the
    family went before Port Pirie held them.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Read off the registers</h2>
    <p class="lede">Each row cites the entry it came from. Nothing here is inferred, and where a
    word is ambiguous it is given as written rather than translated away.</p>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>As written</th><th>In English</th><th>Where</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{reg.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{r.who}</td>
          <td><strong>{r.what}</strong></td>
          <td class="dim">{r.english}</td>
          <td class="dim">{r.place}{r.year ? " · " + r.year : ""}</td>
          <td class="dek" style="font-size:.86em">{r.source}</td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And the {tree.length} the tree carries</h2>
    <p class="lede">Sixteen people out of {W.published.toLocaleString()}. Mostly twentieth-century,
    mostly from family memory rather than a document — and worth showing beside the register rows so
    the difference in kind is visible.</p>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Occupation</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{tree.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{r.slug ? <a href={u("/people/" + r.slug)}>{r.who}</a> : r.who}</td>
          <td>{r.what}</td>
          <td class="dim">{r.place || "—"}</td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The page Hedviga's mother was born on</div>
    <h2>Senj, 1886 — and a man with two trades</h2>
    <p class="lede">The Senj baptisms for 1886 hold
    <a href={u("/people/milka-lucia-papic")}>Milka Lucija Papić</a>, born 9 October. Her father is
    entered <strong><em>trhonoša</em></strong> — a porter, a carrier of burdens.</p>
    <p><strong>A year earlier, on the page that records her sister Marija, the same man is entered
    <em>težak</em></strong> — a labourer. Two words for one man, twelve months apart, written by two
    different chaplains. Neither is wrong; the register is describing what he was doing, not
    awarding him a title, and <strong>this is what it looks like when a man has no trade to
    name</strong>.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">And the ten entries around hers are a cross-section of the town
    that the <a href={u("/senj")}>Senj page</a> has never been able to show:</p>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Father</th><th>As written</th><th>In English</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Marko Kiuvina</td><td><strong>kr. financ. povjerenik</strong></td><td class="dim">royal finance commissioner</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Eduard Malbohan</td><td><strong>kr. šumarnik</strong></td><td class="dim">royal forester</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Vinko Ivan Zanić</td><td><strong>kr. gimnaz. profesor</strong></td><td class="dim">royal gymnasium professor</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ivan Margan</td><td><strong>pilot</strong></td><td class="dim">harbour pilot</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Niko Privosečki</td><td><strong>mešetar</strong></td><td class="dim">broker, middleman</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Franjo Desanti</td><td><strong>bremenar</strong></td><td class="dim">burden-carrier</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Josip Papić</strong></td><td><strong>trhonoša</strong></td><td class="dim">porter</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Vicko Lenac · Jaso Spalj</td><td><strong>težak</strong></td><td class="dim">labourer</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>Three royal officials and a gymnasium professor, on the same
    page as two labourers and two porters.</strong> Senj in 1886 is not a poor town — it is a town
    with an administrative class, and this family is not in it. That is a sharper statement than
    “they were poor”, and it comes from one opening of one book.</p>
    <p class="dek">Two details worth keeping. One family on the page is entered
    <strong><em>grčko-istočne</em></strong> — Greek-Eastern, <strong>Orthodox</strong> — in a
    register otherwise wholly Roman Catholic. And <strong>three of the ten mothers are
    <em>rodj. Prpić</em> or <em>Perpić</em></strong>: Nedela, Ana, and
    <a href={u("/people/antonija-tonka-perpic-prpic")}>Tonka</a>.
    <a href={u("/prpic")}>Krivi Put women were marrying into Senj in numbers.</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">One page, read properly</div>
    <h2>Senj, 1885 — a town of incomers</h2>
    <p class="lede">Page 107 of the Senj baptisms holds eight entries. The
    <em>stalež</em> column is filled in for <strong>every married father on it</strong>, and the
    triage that called the Senj book thin was wrong.</p>

    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Father</th><th>As written</th><th>In English</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Toma Štimac</td><td><strong>nadcestar</strong></td><td class="dim">road overseer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Joso Župan</td><td><strong>bremenar</strong></td><td class="dim">burden-carrier, a porter</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href={u("/people/josip-anton-papic/")}>Josip Papić</a></td><td><strong>težak</strong></td><td class="dim">labourer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Andrija Osterman</td><td><strong>mornar</strong></td><td class="dim">sailor</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.8rem">The godparents on the same page are given
    <em>trgovac</em> (merchant) and <em>postolar</em> (shoemaker). A road overseer, a porter, a
    labourer, a sailor, a merchant and a shoemaker — that is a working port, described in one
    opening.</p>

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <h3>And the column does something else nobody had noticed</h3>
      <p>Where a mother has no trade, the priest writes <strong>where she came from</strong>. Four of
      the eight entries do it: <strong>Jablanac</strong>, the parish of
      <strong>Crnoluška</strong>, the parish of <strong>Starigrad</strong> — and, for Josip Papić's
      wife, <strong><em>Tonka rodj. Prpić <u>iz Krivogputa</u></em></strong>.</p>
      <p>This archive knows <a href={u("/people/antonija-tonka-perpic-prpic/")}>Antonija Prpić</a> is
      of <a href={u("/prpic/")}>Krivi Put</a> because her 1859 baptism is there. <strong>Here the Senj
      register says it too</strong>, twenty-six years later and a mountain away — the town clerk
      recording that this woman is not from the town.</p>
      <p><strong>Senj in 1885 is a town of incomers, and its register says where from.</strong> Four
      parishes in eight entries, all of them in the hinterland or along the coast. The
      <a href={u("/blazevic/")}>Blaževići</a> coming down from Smokvica and the
      <a href={u("/papic/")}>Papići</a> from Selce are not exceptions; they are the pattern, and the
      book was recording it all along.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Added 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2>How the records describe women</h2>
    <p class="lede">Three conventions, from three kinds of document, and they do not agree about
    what a woman's entry is <em>for</em>.</p>

    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Convention</th><th>Example</th><th>What it actually records</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>By her husband's office</strong><br /><span class="dim">Latin registers, Otočac</span></td>
          <td><em>Maria Žubrinić, <strong>mulier Custodis Carcerum</strong></em>, 1845</td>
          <td>The <strong>gaoler's wife</strong>. It names an office, not her trade — and in that
            entry it does not even name the man who holds it. She is identified by a job that is
            not hers.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>By the household</strong><br /><span class="dim">Croatian village registers</span></td>
          <td><em>Martin Kalanj i Marija rodj. Peričić, <strong>polj.</strong></em>, 1898</td>
          <td><strong>Both</strong> entered as <em>poljodjelci</em>, farmers. Here the woman shares
            the description rather than borrowing it — the unit being described is the
            <a href={u("/gazetteer/")}>household</a>, not the man.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>By a category that is not a job</strong><br /><span class="dim">Australian electoral rolls</span></td>
          <td><em>Catherine Zubrinich, <strong>Domestic duties</strong></em>, Broken Hill 1913</td>
          <td>A printed option on a form. Every adult woman in this archive's Australian records
            carries it, whatever she did.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <h3>And then there are the women who are given a trade</h3>
      <p>They exist, and they are worth listing because the conventions above make them almost
      invisible:</p>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li><strong>Olga Štimac</strong> and <strong>Katica Štimac</strong> —
          <em>radnica u tvornici duhana u Senju</em>, <strong>workers in the Senj tobacco
          factory</strong>, named at a 1910 marriage</li>
        <li>A <strong>supervisor of workers at the cigarette factory</strong>, Senj — the only
          supervisory role recorded for anybody in this archive, man or woman</li>
        <li><em>Domaćica i šnajder</em> — <strong>housewife <em>and</em> seamstress</strong>; the
          entry insists on both</li>
        <li><em>Seljakina</em> — a peasant woman, in her own right rather than her husband's</li>
        <li><em>Medicinska sestra</em> — a nurse</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="dek">The Senj tobacco factory turns up repeatedly, and it is the one institution in
      these records that employed women by name. It still stands on Ul. Stjepana Radića as a textile
      works. <a href={u("/senj/")}>Senj →</a></p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>Why this matters for the counting.</strong> Of the
    {W.rows.length} occupations here, the ones attached to women are mostly not occupations at all —
    they are relationships or form-fields. Any statement this archive makes about <em>what this
    family did</em> is therefore a statement about its men, and should be read that way until the
    tobacco-factory records are looked for.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What this is not, yet</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>It is not a survey.</strong> Thirty-eight records against
      {" "}{W.published.toLocaleString()} people. Every one had to be read off a scan by hand, and
      <a href={u("/searched/")}>twenty-seven register pages this archive already holds</a> have not
      been read for this column at all.</li>
      <li><strong>It is the form of the register, not its language.</strong> This was assumed the
      other way round and checked on 13 September 2026. The <strong>ruled tabular</strong> books
      carry a <em>conditio</em> column — Otočac 1834, 1835 and 1845 give a trade or a rank to
      <em>every parent and every godparent</em>, which is why almost everything above comes from
      three pages. The <strong>prose</strong> books have no column at all: Karlobag 1754–1781 runs
      as continuous Latin sentences, and <strong>six of the eight Latin documents this archive holds
      are those</strong>. Reading them for occupations is wasted effort. Croatian tabular books sit
      in between — Novi Vinodolski names the parents' trade but usually not the godparents', and
      Sv. Jakov Krmpote mostly writes <em>seljaci</em> and stops.</li>
      <li><strong>Women are recorded three different ways, and only one of them is work.</strong>
      See below.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><a class="btn" href={u("/frontier/")}>The Frontier →</a>
      {" "}<a class="btn" href={u("/zubrinic/")}>Žubrinić →</a>
      {" "}<a class="btn" href={u("/searched/")}>What has been searched →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
